 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Authorizes public authorities and public benefit corporations to negotiate with professional firms providing architectural or engineering services in order from the most qualified to the least qualified with regard to the provision of services to the authority or corporation.",
 "title": "Requires public authorities to negotiate with most qualified architectural and engineering professional firms before negotiating with other firms",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "S5600",
     "basePrintNoStr": "S5600-2017",
     "printNo": "S5600",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 228\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 5600, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to requir-\n      ing public authorities and public benefit corporations to  negoti-\n      ate with professional firms providing architectural or engineering\n      services  in  order from the most qualified to the least qualified\n      with regard to the provision  of  services  to  the  authority  or\n      corporation\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  Quality-based  selection  (QBS) is a federally-established procurement\nprocess which primarily  evaluates  professional  service  providers  by\nquality  rather  than estimated project costs. This bill would amend the\nPublic Authorities Law to require all of the State's public  authorities\nand  public benefit corporations to use the QBS methodology when procur-\ning professional architectural, engineering and surveying services.  For\neach  project,  the  public  authority would be required to select three\nfirms, and then commence negotiations with the top firm on the list.  If\nno  agreement  is  reached,  then the public authority would move to the\nnext firm.\n \n  I am committed to ensuring that New York receives the  best  available\nservices  for  vital  projects  across the State. Best value procurement\nprovides authorities with the ability to obtain the best possible result\nfor its projects and the use of taxpayer dollars. Mandating that  public\nauthorities  utilize  a  specific  procurement  process,  however, would\nnecessarily restrict the State's  ability  to  negotiate  for  the  best\nvalue. Because this bill would remove the ability for public authorities\nto  engage  in  best value procurement for these services, it would have\nthe potential to increase State costs and limit the ability  of  new  or\ndiverse firms to have access to important contracts.\n \n  Finally, it may be challenging for public authorities and public bene-\nfit corporations to identify and select three firms in projects in rural\nareas or where there is a need for very specialized services, thus caus-\ning  unnecessary  delay  to  important public works projects.  For these\nreasons, I am constrained to veto the bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
